{
  "prompt": "Q: A retired plumber from Ohio claims he invented a self-cooling beer can in 1973. Is this likely true, and what would have to be different about thermodynamics for it to work?\nA:",
  "text": " No, but the patent office has not been very rigorous on requiring evidence of prior art before granting patents (in fact that is a legal standard). I don't know if there are any other issues with the \"self cooling\" can besides heat transfer (i.e., maybe it's too heavy or something), so let me tackle those here first since they seem more important than how you'd actually get the cold into the can without adding additional energy inputs like ice cubes. For an ideal case where we're just dealing with conduction between two objects at equilibrium temperatures T1 = T2 via their common boundary surface area A and thermal conductivity k, Fourier's law tells us Q/t = -k * dT/dx , which simplifies down quite nicely when our system becomes one-dimensional along x direction only",
